G3D(3)                         P32W Unix                          G3D(3)

NAME
       g3d - three dimensions in lines, for a program that draws

SYNOPSIS
       #include <g3d.h>

       void g3clear(void);
       int  g3line(int x0, int y0, int z0, int x1, int y1, int z1);
       void g3box(int x0, int y0, int z0, int x1, int y1, int z1);
       void g3turn(int ax, int ay, int az);
       void g3put(int x, int y, int z);
       void g3view(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1, int d);
       int  g3seen(short out[][4], int max);

       cc prog.c -lgfx

DESCRIPTION
       A world is a list of lines between places of whole
       numbers: x to the right, y up, z away from the eye. The
       world is turned about its own middle (where its numbers
       begin) and then put somewhere in front of the eye, which
       looks along z. What the eye sees falls on a rectangle of
       the picture gfx(3) draws.

       All of it is in the header, as P8X's library of the same
       name is put into the programs that use it: include it in
       the one source file of a program. There is no library to
       name to cc but the graphics library's.

       g3clear     empties the world.
       g3line      puts a line in it: 0, or -1 when it has its
                   512 already.
       g3box       puts in the twelve edges of a box, between two
                   opposite corners.
       g3turn      turns the world: ay degrees about its upright
                   axis, then tipped ax about the axis from side
                   to side, then az about the line of sight. Each
                   call turns it from where it was made, not from
                   where the last call left it.
       g3put       puts the world's middle x to the right of the
                   eye, y above it and z in front of it.
       g3view      says which rectangle of the picture the world
                   is seen in (two opposite corners, in dots),
                   and how far the eye is from it, in dots:
                   something d across and d away just fills d
                   dots.
       g3seen      works out the lines as they fall on the
                   picture: each that shows, cut to the
                   rectangle, is four numbers of out, x0 y0 x1
                   y1, max lines at most. It returns how many.
                   What is behind the eye is not seen.

       Nothing is drawn by these. A program draws the lines of
       g3seen with gline, and to move the world draws the old
       ones again in the ink that turns dots over (G_XOR) before
       the new ones.

EXAMPLES
              short l[16][4];
              int n, k;
              g3clear();
              g3box(-90, -90, -90, 90, 90, 90);
              g3view(0, 0, gwidth - 1, gheight - 1, gheight);
              g3put(0, 0, 540);
              g3turn(20, 30, 0);
              n = g3seen(l, 16);
              for (k = 0; k < n; k++)
                  gline(l[k][0], l[k][1], l[k][2], l[k][3]);

FILES
       /usr/include/g3d.h
       /usr/src/cmd/cube.c, /usr/src/cmd/house.c
                                  programs that use it

SEE ALSO
       gfx(3), cube(1), house(1)

BUGS
       Lines only: nothing hides what is behind it.
       The numbers are whole ones of 32 bits. A world within
       8000 of its middle each way, and nearer than 30000, is
       safe.
       Two lines in the same place, drawn in the ink that turns
       dots over, are no line.
